65f9ff3","f9ef5c1dce9105cc","fa48debc5ca00dca","fa8e46f55ad75864","fb10abe1078a8b0c","fb4ec3ac89469d2f","fb8b15ca780ec16f","fbee0e320c3acd1f","fbf5dcb3d2af7a66","fc07dbe8eecf0efe","fc323b7fcd637b90","fc3482c42e5a6a02","fc3dd6352bf14344","fc90c4b75f0bfe4f","fcf337e475070ad0","fd3d1359c30c55eb","fdbcb936ec9ce1f5","fdcdf7e816d26410","fdfff7a436622fdf","fe30ec36d85a0528","fe37435eead2daf5","fe45fd024d123bc2","fe57d0842bac6074","fe8515e82c30bef5","fea6e8f0f814496a","fec667e46d55e7c5","fee513c7d1425c8f","ff3feaf551204b53","ff84db14c3aa0121","ffa9d71b57a39f26","ffb98efc4a87963d","ffcba41ffe46b311","fff4dbc6731f8f2f"],"groups":["2fbd42edd7943ddf","91959db375cf21a0","22b2c25d2c82bf16","54416482276cee4f","a71e0f5a10f0097f","1910e9044f6ce19f","4490df430a693b8e","a91fe9ec7da378be","1c233d8479fefc3b","a3bf604b5d4add50","d523a0b50a99feb0","c308bb657ac612ee","f5ad07689903ca40","37d0841f59ea2c70"],"findings":"**Review of the \"Review desk\" rewrite (snapshot 3f36269)**\n\nI ran the attention and gloss suites: 97 pass, 0 fail, 0 skipped, so the Chromium tests ran. I also built a fixture page and loaded it in Chromium. Findings are tagged **verified** (I ran it) or **read** (from the code).\n\n**1. Mismatches**\n\n- **tests (group `tests`): the `#g-config` hash assertion is vacuous.** *Verified.* The test in `test/gloss.test.ts` loads `#g-config` with storage disabled and asserts only `/aria-current=\"true\"/`. I built the same two-group fixture and loaded the page with no hash: the default selection is `retry-core` and it also carries `aria-current=\"true\"`. So the regex passes whether or not the hash is honoured. The test does not show that \"the group in the URL hash is selected\", which is what its message claims. Assert the selected `h1.intent` text instead.\n- **read-progress / range-order: `#overview` hash in a range.** *Read.* `applyHash` maps `#overview` to `selectKind(0, 'overview')`, which is the newest commit in history order. The initial selection on a range is `commitOrder()[0]`, which is the hottest commit when hot ordering is on. A reload or link to `#overview` on a range lands on a different commit than the default view.\n- **read-progress / range-order: `j` stops at the end of a commit.** *Read.* In group mode, `stepTo` only calls `neighbor()`, which returns null at the last visible group. It never moves to the next commit in the range, though the navigation docs say `j`/`k` step commits on a range. This needs a check in the browser before anyone relies on it.\n- **desk-layout: filters do not re-select the pane.** *Read.* `renderChrome` refreshes only the rail, the top bar and the nav. If a filter hides the open group, the pane keeps showing it, and the rail no longer marks it selected. This is inconsistent with `selectGroup`, which resets filters to reveal a group.\n\n**2. Misgraded necessity**\n\n- **read-progress [required]: should not be required.** The task is \"reimplement the page as the Review desk master-detail design\". Read marks, progress and the `r` key are, by the author's own summary, \"new behaviour beyond the old page\". The \"if removed\" text cites an \"approved design\", which is not part of the stated task. It is at most supporting or speculative, and it is the largest removable piece (about 40 lines of storage code, plus tests).\n- **range-order [required]: same concern.** The summary says it is new behaviour. Its notes admit it changes the existing default ordering of range pages. Required is a stretch.\n- **tests [supporting]:** the group is reasonable. The storage-less test, though, does not check what it claims (see point 1). *Verified:* `--disable-local-storage` does make `localStorage` access throw in this Chromium, so the blocked-storage path is exercised.\n\n**3. Removal candidates**\n\n- **read-progress**: dropping it removes the progress bar, checkboxes, `r` key, and `readSet`/`setRead`/`readCounts`. Impact: no read tracking. Everything else still works, since `markRead` is only used by this group. Deferring it keeps the rewrite smaller and avoids localStorage on `file://`, which the author flags as shared across pages with the same sha.\n- **range-order**: deferring hottest-first keeps history order as the default, which is the existing behaviour. The toggle and the strip can come later. Impact: less at-a-glance triage on large ranges.\n\n**4. Simplifications**\n\n- `reviewOrder` and its test are clean and I checked the expected ordering by hand. No change needed.\n- The duplicated `'#risks'` and `review` class logic is fine. `reviewClass` is equivalent to the old expression.\n- Consider asserting the selected group's title in the storage-less test instead of the bare `aria-current`.\n\n**5. Missing risks**\n\n- The `'overview'` hash mismatch and the `j` stop at a commit boundary (above).\n- Filter changes leave a hidden group in the pane (above).\n- **Verified:** the default selection for a single commit is the first group in review order, and the URL hash overrides it only when it names a group. Behaviour is consistent with the author's description, so this is not a bug, but the tests do not pin it down.\n\nThe author's annotations on the code are otherwise accurate. I checked the `codeOpen` default change, the `o/e/c` global flag, the `reviewClass` equivalence, and the `reviewOrder` tests. The claim that the Chromium hover-contrast test fails without the hover rule, and the `refreshNav` claim, I did not run.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
