de59810a4a","f8463a634ada0577","f857e846f504caaf","f8988c520a222a34","f8b91347bfb5210e","f90f2465d53ecc6b","f935007006bba746","f93bb75529cceb61","f948ef92fb885681","f94d560229584c4e","f9553a1d2a9f589e","f97c3dea80fb02d9","f982558f7608f27d","f99bdc5c2a8fce93","f9b47e6f965f9ff3","f9cda0faf74dfc59","f9d39d39a07be7f6","f9ef5c1dce9105cc","fa48debc5ca00dca","fa80601736a24cac","fa8e46f55ad75864","fae9c7b8a7f40dd6","faf5c5de97b8ab69","fb10abe1078a8b0c","fb4b1c1b8d5164e1","fb4ec3ac89469d2f","fb595f9bcc3d1b42","fb8b15ca780ec16f","fba7b325989d0f31","fbe5608d35db3522","fbee0e320c3acd1f","fbf5dcb3d2af7a66","fc07dbe8eecf0efe","fc323b7fcd637b90","fc3482c42e5a6a02","fc3dd6352bf14344","fc90c4b75f0bfe4f","fcf337e475070ad0","fd3d1359c30c55eb","fd48c1798ee1923a","fd6896c08424f3d9","fd6b035dd8f9c3e2","fdbcb936ec9ce1f5","fdcdf7e816d26410","fdfff7a436622fdf","fe30ec36d85a0528","fe45fd024d123bc2","fe4f7bd35e5e063a","fe4fc77dbf2b842d","fe57d0842bac6074","fe6dd8fe6091842e","fe80d3f52835dc72","fe8515e82c30bef5","fe988b55f9ee10ee","fea6e8f0f814496a","fec667e46d55e7c5","fed63d5f54236026","fee513c7d1425c8f","ff1b71a5b336db04","ff84db14c3aa0121","ffa9d71b57a39f26","ffb98efc4a87963d","ffcba41ffe46b311","ffcfde292959f22f","fff054db5e71dc02","fff4dbc6731f8f2f"],"groups":["b3909996345e5bfa","becd89c6b952c141","65c802244b45de0c","f2b1569217bafef3","54416482276cee4f","c117f285f04a3717","1910e9044f6ce19f","4490df430a693b8e","1c233d8479fefc3b","f5ad07689903ca40","3c5424f4b42df570","ce0f77c7c3d71517","ff94ae8be8d0b5c5","35187ef7e0b16c0c","37d0841f59ea2c70"],"findings":"**Scope and method.** I read the diff and annotations and spot-checked a few claims against the code. I did not run the test suite, the browser checks, or the screenshots, so anything below marked **read** is from reading only. The sandbox has Node 26.7.0, which is why I could not reproduce the Node 20 skip behaviour directly.\n\n**1. Mismatch / missing risk: the XSS browser test silently stops running on Node 20 (read).**\n`test/gloss.test.ts:437` (\"a hostile gloss renders as inert text in a real browser\") changed from `skip: !chromium` to `skip: skipBrowse`. `skipBrowse` in `test/browser.ts` also skips when the global `WebSocket` is missing. `package.json` declares `\"node\": \">=20.12\"`, and Node 20 has no global WebSocket. So on Node 20 the hostile-gloss test, the one that guards the nonce CSP and text-only rendering, no longer runs. Before this commit it ran on any Node with Chromium. The `tests` group notes say skips happen \"with a message\", but that is not in the missing-risks list, and the test is not mentioned as a coverage loss. Either restore a non-WebSocket path for this assertion (for example, keep `--dump-dom`, which the old test used) or call out the coverage drop.\n\n**2. Misgraded necessity: `desk-layout` [required] bundles behaviour changes the task did not ask for (read).**\nThe task is \"reimplement as the Review desk master-detail design\". The group also changes:\n- folding from per-group to per-hunk, with in-memory fold state (`hunkClosed`);\n- the meaning of `o`, `e` and `c`;\n- the `r` key;\n- a separate Risks and alternatives page, reached from a rail row and an attention-card link.\n\nThe author lists these as behaviour changes, but grades the whole group as required. Some of this may be what the approved design says, which I cannot check. If the design does not cover it, the per-hunk fold and the risks page should be split out and graded `supporting` or `incidental`, so they can be dropped without losing the desk layout.\n\n**3. Mismatch: line-range attribution is approximate, but the claims read as exact (read).**\nThe `read-progress` and `range-order` groups claim specific line ranges inside the very large `client.js` hunks h2 and h3. The risks list admits these are \"approximate, not exact attribution\". The annotations therefore cannot be used to say which lines belong to which group. The hunk-level grading is fine, but the line-level claims should be marked as approximate in the groups themselves.\n\n**4. Misgraded necessity: parts of `tests` [supporting] are incidental (read).**\n- Moving the browser helper to `test/browser.ts` is needed by the new browser tests, so it is supporting.\n- Rewriting the hostile-gloss test to use the new helper (`test/gloss.test.ts` h18) is not needed. The old `--dump-dom` version worked. This is the change that causes finding 1.\n- The `skipBrowse` WebSocket gate is a drive-by change for Node 20.\n\nRemoval candidate: revert the hostile-gloss test to its previous mechanism. Impact: none on the desk feature; restores coverage on Node 20.\n\n**5. Verified by reading: `review-order` [supporting] is accurate and lean.**\n`reviewOrder` matches its comment. I checked the expected arrays in `reviewOrder` test in `test/gloss.test.ts` by hand: `['d','b','e','a','f','c']` and `['c','a','d','b','e','f']` follow from the rank, size and index rules. The client uses `order` in `orderedGroups`, `neighbor` and `groupList`, so `serves: desk-layout` is correct.\n\n**6. Verified by reading: `range-order` default and ordering match the code.**\n`commitOrder` reverses `data.commits` (oldest first) and sorts by level only when `state.hot`, which defaults to `true`. This matches the notes.\n\n**7. Unverified claims (not run).**\nThe `tests` group says several new tests fail when their fix is removed, and the `read-progress` group says the storage-blocked path is covered. I did not run these, so they are unconfirmed.\n\n**8. Simplification.** `desk-layout` duplicates the impact and attention badge logic across `groupPane`, `focusSection` and `groupRow` (each sets `--m` or `--lv` and a title). A small helper would cut the repetition. Low priority.\n\n**Summary.** The `review-order` and `range-order` annotations are accurate. The main problems are the lost Node 20 coverage for the XSS test (finding 1), `desk-layout` being graded required while it carries unrequested behaviour changes (finding 2), and approximate line claims presented without that caveat (finding 3).\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
