he trailing VERDICT line, and `verdictOf` does that over the last 400 characters. It is consistent with the notes. A report that mentions \"VERDICT: CLEAN\" in its last 400 characters before a later line would be misread, but that is an edge case, not a mismatch.\n- **Minor (read).** In the `keydown` handler, `var t` is declared twice in one function (once for `ev.target`, again in the `o` branch). It is harmless because the second assignment comes after the earlier check, but it is misleading.\n\n### 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **`range-order` as `required` (read).** Its own summary calls it \"new behaviour beyond the old page\" and its notes say it \"changes the existing default\". Changing the default range order from oldest-first is a design choice, not something the Review-desk task requires by itself. Unless the approved design explicitly asks for hottest-first, this should be `supporting` or `speculative`, with the default change called out.\n- **`read-progress` as `required` (read).** Same question. The notes call it \"New behaviour, not in the old page\", and the justification rests on an \"approved design\" the reviewer cannot see. Keep it as `required` only if that design is cited. Otherwise it is new scope.\n- **`review-order` as `supporting` (read).** This is correct. The rail depends on `order`, and `order` is only used by `orderedGroups`.\n- **`tests` as `supporting` (read).** It is a large set of hunks (h13–h23) and bundles new behaviour tests with the test-helper refactor. The helper move is `mechanical`, and the new tests are `supporting` for their groups. Splitting it would make the claims easier to check.\n\n### 3. Removal candidates\n\n- None of the required groups is clearly removable. The `read-progress` group (about 7 hunks) is the one to drop or defer if the scope is trimmed. Dropping it would also drop the read-storage tests and the `r` key, and the footer and top-bar text would need updating.\n- `range-order`'s default change could be reduced to a History-order default with the hottest-first toggle kept. That keeps the old range order and removes the one visible default change.\n\n### 4. Simplifications\n\n- `honestyLabel` repeats `reviewClass` and `reviewShort` logic. A single helper for the \"stage state\" label would shrink it.\n- The keyboard `o` branch could call the same toggle helper as the button click, so the logic lives in one place.\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Read marks are lost when the tab closes (listed by the author, but worth weighing).** For a reviewer, marks that vanish on a closed tab are a real usability gap. The author lists it, so this is not hidden.\n- **Hunk-to-group attribution is approximate (listed).** The per-group line ranges are rough, so any \"per-group\" change size or necessity figure derived from them is not exact.\n- **Risks shown only in the Overview (read).** On a single-commit page, a reviewer who is in a group pane must go back to the Overview to see risks. The rail link covers this, but it is a change from the old page, where risks sat next to the groups.\n- **Focus test coverage (verified in part).** The keyboard-focus test passes. I did not verify the claim that it fails with the focus restore disabled.\n\n### Summary\n\nThe layout, order and review claims match the code, and the suite passes. The storage claims are overstated: the storage-blocked tests do not block `sessionStorage`, so the in-memory fallback has no coverage. Also, `range-order` and `read-progress` are labelled `required` although the author's own notes describe them as new behaviour beyond the old page.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
