f93bb75529cceb61","f948ef92fb885681","f94d560229584c4e","f9553a1d2a9f589e","f9b47e6f965f9ff3","f9ef5c1dce9105cc","fa48debc5ca00dca","fa80601736a24cac","fa8e46f55ad75864","fae9c7b8a7f40dd6","faf5c5de97b8ab69","fb10abe1078a8b0c","fb4ec3ac89469d2f","fb595f9bcc3d1b42","fb8b15ca780ec16f","fbe5608d35db3522","fbee0e320c3acd1f","fbf5dcb3d2af7a66","fc07dbe8eecf0efe","fc323b7fcd637b90","fc3482c42e5a6a02","fc3dd6352bf14344","fc90c4b75f0bfe4f","fcea70023e154214","fcf337e475070ad0","fd3d1359c30c55eb","fdbcb936ec9ce1f5","fdcdf7e816d26410","fdfff7a436622fdf","fe30ec36d85a0528","fe37435eead2daf5","fe45fd024d123bc2","fe4f7bd35e5e063a","fe4fc77dbf2b842d","fe57d0842bac6074","fe8515e82c30bef5","fe988b55f9ee10ee","fea6e8f0f814496a","fec667e46d55e7c5","fee513c7d1425c8f","ff3feaf551204b53","ff84db14c3aa0121","ffa9d71b57a39f26","ffb98efc4a87963d","ffcba41ffe46b311","fff4dbc6731f8f2f"],"groups":["2fbd42edd7943ddf","91959db375cf21a0","22b2c25d2c82bf16","f2b1569217bafef3","54416482276cee4f","819345d2273595f3","1910e9044f6ce19f","4490df430a693b8e","a91fe9ec7da378be","1c233d8479fefc3b","a3bf604b5d4add50","d523a0b50a99feb0","c308bb657ac612ee","f5ad07689903ca40","3c5424f4b42df570","ce0f77c7c3d71517","37d0841f59ea2c70"],"findings":"**Verification status.** I checked the hunk line counts against `git show --stat HEAD` (attention.test.ts +91/−5, reviews.test.ts +51/−6, gloss.test.ts +22/−1 plus +21, data.ts +17, all consistent with the hunk table). I could not run the test suite: `gloss.test.ts` exceeded my 100s limit, and the background run of both files exited 0 with empty output. Everything below marked **read** comes from reading the code. Nothing is marked **verified** beyond the line counts.\n\n## 1. Mismatches\n\n- **`reviews-view` evidence (read):** The cited screenshots are of `f92caa7` and `2107f50`, earlier commits, not of this change. The rendering was rewritten, so the evidence does not cover the code under review.\n- **`read-progress` evidence (read):** It says \"No automated test of the marks themselves.\" Accurate. But the `.nav` / Previous-Next state test in attention.test.ts exercises the filter and position logic that `read-progress` shares, so the group's \"no test\" claim understates coverage.\n- **`review-order` serves (read):** It lists only `desk-layout`. `c.order` also drives `orderedGroups`, which backs j/k stepping (`stepTo`/`neighbor`) and the \"Group N of M\" crumb, so it also serves `read-progress` and `range-order`.\n- **`reviewOrder` comment (read):** \"a stable sort over the groups as they come\" is not quite right. The sort is explicit on index `a.i` as a tiebreak, which is correct. The comment should say so.\n\nAccurate claims I could confirm by reading: `codeOpen: true` by default, `o/e/c` as one global flag via `setCode`, no `style` attributes or `innerHTML` in the client (colours go through `style.setProperty`), and `el()` uses `textContent`.\n\n## 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **`read-progress` [required] → should be supporting or deferrable (read).** The task is a layout reimplementation. The group's own intent calls read marks \"New behaviour, not in the old page\", and the task statement does not mention them. The rail still works with the marks removed. The \"approved design\" justification is not visible in the material. Removing it drops the checkboxes, progress bar, `r` key and \"mark read and go to next\" button, plus the localStorage code (about 40 lines in client.js), and nothing else breaks.\n- **`range-order` [required] → should be incidental or deferrable (read).** The notes admit it \"changes the existing default\" from history order to hottest-first. That is a visible behaviour change the task did not ask for. Removing it keeps history order and loses the strip summary and toggle. Also, the evidence is \"checked by hand\", with no automated test.\n- **`reviews-view` [required] → half-required (read).** Moving reviews into a separate main-pane view is a design choice, and it is defensible as part of the desk layout. The hand-rolled markdown renderer (`inline`/`markdown`, roughly 70 lines) is a separate, unrequested feature: findings were already shown as text. It should be its own group, labelled incidental, with its own tests. Removing it would only leave findings as plain pre-wrapped text, which the old page already did.\n- **`tests` [supporting]** is fine. Its `serves` list correctly names the groups it covers.\n\n## 3. Removal candidates\n\n- `read-progress` (about 40 lines plus the `r` key, the progress bar and the nav button). Impact: loss of read tracking, which the author states is new anyway.\n- `range-order`'s hot toggle and strip (about 50 lines). Impact: history order only. The `commitOrder` helper stays, so the change is small.\n- Markdown renderer (about 70 lines). Impact: findings render as text. The `markdown` test assertions in reviews.test.ts would go too.\n\n## 4. Simplifications\n\n- `reviewOrder` can drop the `a.i` tiebreak if `Array.prototype.sort` stability is relied on. Node's sort is stable, so this is a readability choice only. Keeping it explicit is fine.\n- `levelOf` and `honestyLabel` both map verdict/state to a class in similar ways. `reviewClass` already does this, and `honestyLabel` could reuse it. Minor.\n\n## 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Browser test flakiness (read):** `browse()` in attention.test.ts picks a random port in 9300–9899 with no collision check. It kills Chrome without waiting for exit. It also depends on the global `WebSocket`, which needs Node 22 or later. Expect intermittent failures in CI. I could not confirm this by running it.\n- **Test runtime (read):** The browser-driven tests each launch a fresh Chrome. My `gloss.test.ts` run exceeded 100 seconds, so the suite is slow. That is a practical risk for the author's workflow.\n- **Hash-selected group when storage is blocked (read):** The storage-less test covers the hash path, but not the case where a read mark exists for a group that was later renamed. That case is covered by the listed \"re-gloss reuses id\" risk, so it is noted but not new.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
