798ee1923a","fd6b035dd8f9c3e2","fd6b035dd8f9c3e2","fdbcb936ec9ce1f5","fdcdf7e816d26410","fdfff7a436622fdf","fe30ec36d85a0528","fe37435eead2daf5","fe45fd024d123bc2","fe4f7bd35e5e063a","fe4fc77dbf2b842d","fe57d0842bac6074","fe80d3f52835dc72","fe8515e82c30bef5","fe988b55f9ee10ee","fea6e8f0f814496a","fec667e46d55e7c5","fed63d5f54236026","fee513c7d1425c8f","ff84db14c3aa0121","ffa9d71b57a39f26","ffb98efc4a87963d","ffcba41ffe46b311","fff054db5e71dc02","fff4dbc6731f8f2f"],"groups":["a6b0b2c7a1010ca8","becd89c6b952c141","65c802244b45de0c","f2b1569217bafef3","caa2a6839954fee6","6b3a8a3d710cc3b8","1910e9044f6ce19f","4490df430a693b8e","1c233d8479fefc3b","f5ad07689903ca40","3c5424f4b42df570","ce0f77c7c3d71517","ff94ae8be8d0b5c5","35187ef7e0b16c0c","37d0841f59ea2c70"],"findings":"**Mismatches**\n\n1. **desk-layout / review-order: \"Largest first · by changed lines\" uses a different measure from the review order.** *read.* The client sorts \"Largest first\" by `weight`, which is a share-weighted count where lines claimed by several groups are split between them (`src/html/data.ts:215`). The data's review-order tie-break uses `added + removed` (`src/html/data.ts` `reviewOrder`, via `counts`). For groups that share lines these give different orders, so the same page can rank groups by size in two different ways. The author's \"by changed lines\" describes neither precisely. Fix: use one measure, or say which one.\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n\n2. **range-order [required]: this changes existing behaviour the task did not ask for.** *read, backed by the author's own notes.* The old range page listed commits oldest first. This group switches the rail to newest first, makes hottest-first the default, adds a History order toggle, a strip, and a \"Read first\" link. The task is a redesign. The author's own summary calls these \"new behaviour beyond the old page\". Without this group the rail would still work in history order, so \"required\" is hard to support. It is better graded incidental or speculative, with the default change flagged as a deliberate decision.\n\n3. **read-progress [required]: \"the approved design\" is not in the task statement.** *read.* The stated task is to reimplement the page as the Review desk master-detail design. The summary calls read marks new behaviour. If the design brief does mandate them, \"required\" is fair, but that should be cited. Otherwise this is speculative or supporting.\n\n4. **desk-layout [required] bundles a feature the author also calls new.** *read.* The Order control (four modes, plus the `groupOrder` size/file/gloss branches, `ORDERS`, and `orderSeg`) is listed inside the required layout group, though the notes call it new behaviour. The layout does not need it.\n\n**Removal candidates**\n\n5. **Order control, with `review-order`'s `glossOrder`.** *read.* `glossOrder` exists only to serve the \"Gloss order\" option. Dropping the Order control (keeping review order alone) removes about 30 lines of client code, the `glossOrder` field, and the order assertions in the last test. The desk design itself loses nothing it needs.\n\n6. **range-order** (see item 2). Dropping it, or reducing it to history order newest first, loses hottest-first triage and the strip. That is a real loss of function, so it is a product decision rather than a clear cut.\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n7. The hand-rolled markdown renderer is about 80 lines. It is justified by the \"raw text in scroll boxes\" problem the group describes, so I would keep it. The notes list emphasis and links as supported, which matches the code (`inline`, `markdown` in `client.js`).\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n8. *read.* Every hunk of the selected group is now built eagerly by `hunkView`. Before, hunks were built only on \"Show code\". The unannotated pane also builds all its hunks at once. Each selection rebuilds the whole pane. This is probably fine for one commit, but it is untested on a large range or a large unannotated commit.\n\n9. *read.* `syncFold` disables both Expand and Collapse when a group has no toggleable hunks (for example, only missing hunks), because `0 === 0` makes both conditions true. This is an edge case, and the buttons should still be sensible there.\n\n**Verified**\n\n- *verified.* I ran `node --test` on `test/attention.test.ts`, `test/reviews.test.ts` and `test/gloss.test.ts` with Node 26 and the system Chromium. Result: 121 tests, 121 pass, 0 skipped. So the browser tests run here and pass, and the \"existing browser tests pass\" evidence holds.\n- *read.* I did not check the author's claim that each new test fails when its fix is removed. The \"fails without X\" evidence for read-progress and the hover contrast test is unverified.\n- *read.* The hunk-range attributions for read-progress and range-order are approximate, as the author says. I did not check them line by line.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
